.TH INIT 8 2026-09-25 "Substrate" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
init \- process control initialization
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B /sbin/init
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B init
is the first user process, started by the kernel as process 1 once the
root filesystem is mounted.
It runs the boot scripts, keeps a
.BR getty (8)
running on each console terminal, records boot, shutdown and console
logouts in the utmp and wtmp files, and shuts the system down when signalled.
It takes no arguments and reads no configuration file; any arguments are
ignored.
The kernel mounts
.IR /proc ,
.I /sys
and
.I /dev
itself, so
.B init
does not.
.SS Startup
.IP 1. 3
.I /dev/console
is opened and duplicated onto standard input, output and error.
.IP 2. 3
.B SIGHUP
is ignored, and the console is released as the controlling terminal with
.B TIOCNOTTY
so that a
.B getty
can later claim
.IR /dev/tty1 .
.IP 3. 3
Handlers are installed for the shutdown signals (see
.BR SIGNALS ).
.IP 4. 3
The directories
.IR /var ,
.I /var/run
and
.I /var/log
are created if missing.
.IP 5. 3
A
.B BOOT_TIME
record (user
.BR reboot ,
line
.BR ~ )
is appended to
.IR /var/log/wtmp ,
and
.I /var/run/utmp
is truncated and rewritten with that single record.
.IP 6. 3
.B "/bin/sh /etc/rc start"
is run, and
.B init
waits for it to finish before going on.
.IP 7. 3
A
.B /sbin/getty
is started on each of
.IR /dev/tty1 ,
.I /dev/tty2
and
.IR /dev/tty3 .
This table is compiled in.
Each getty runs in a new session, with the default action restored for
.BR SIGTERM ,
.BR SIGINT ,
.B SIGQUIT
and
.BR SIGCHLD .
.PP
.B init
then loops, once a second reaping exited children.
When the process started on a terminal line exits, the session on that line
has ended:
.B init
writes a
.B DEAD_PROCESS
record for the line to utmp and wtmp and starts a new getty there.
A line is not restarted more than once per second.
.SS The boot scripts
.I /etc/rc
takes one argument,
.BR start ,
.BR stop ,
.B restart
or
.BR status .
It runs every executable file in
.I /etc/rc.d
with that argument, in lexical order of the file names, or in reverse order
for
.BR stop ,
printing each script name and action first.
A numeric prefix in the file name sets the order.
The scripts shipped are:
.TP
.B 00-fsck
Check the root filesystem with
.B "e2fsck \-p"
if it is mounted read-only, then remount it read-write.
If
.B e2fsck
leaves uncorrected errors, the root stays read-only; if it repaired the
filesystem, the system is rebooted before the remount.
.TP
.B 00-hostname
Set the host name from
.IR /etc/hostname .
.TP
.BR 01-run-clean ", " 02-tmp-clean
Remove stale runtime state from
.I /var/run
and
.IR /tmp .
.TP
.B 05-syslogd
Start
.BR syslogd (8).
.TP
.B 06-dbus
Start the system D-Bus daemon.
.TP
.B 10-loopback
Configure the loopback interface.
.TP
.BR 15-rpcbind ", " 16-ttdbserver
Start the RPC port mapper and the ToolTalk database server.
.TP
.B 20-network
Configure interfaces from
.IR /etc/network.conf ,
running
.BR dhclient (8)
for interfaces marked
.BR dhcp .
.TP
.B 30-atd
Start
.BR atd (8).
.TP
.B 35-telnetd
Start
.BR telnetd (8).
.TP
.BR 40-inetd ", " 45-sshd ", " 50-nginx
Start inetd, sshd and nginx.
.TP
.B 60-sdm
Start the graphical login,
.BR sdm (8),
if it and the X server are installed.
.PP
Daemon scripts share helpers from
.IR /etc/rc.subr ,
which is sourced, not executed.
.SS Shutdown
On a shutdown signal
.B init
stops restarting gettys and:
.IP 1. 3
sends
.B SIGHUP
and
.B SIGTERM
to each getty session's process group and leader, waits up to 3 seconds
for them to exit, then sends
.B SIGKILL
to whatever is left;
.IP 2. 3
runs
.BR "/bin/sh /etc/rc stop" ,
waiting for it at most 15 seconds;
.IP 3. 3
sends
.B SIGHUP
and then
.B SIGTERM
to every process, waits up to 5 seconds for its children to exit,
then sends
.B SIGKILL
to every process;
.IP 4. 3
appends a
.B RUN_LVL
record (user
.BR shutdown ,
line
.BR ~ )
to wtmp, calls
.BR sync (2),
and calls
.BR reboot (2)
to halt, reboot or power off.
.PP
If
.BR reboot (2)
returns,
.B init
sleeps forever.
.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.BR SIGTERM ", " SIGQUIT
Shut down and halt.
.TP
.B SIGINT
Shut down and reboot (the Ctrl-Alt-Del convention).
.TP
.B SIGUSR1
Shut down and power off.
.TP
.B SIGUSR2
Shut down and reboot.
.TP
.B SIGHUP
Ignored.
.PP
The
.BR halt ,
.B reboot
and
.B poweroff
commands in
.I /sbin
send
.BR SIGTERM ,
.B SIGUSR2
and
.B SIGUSR1
respectively to process 1.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /dev/console
Standard input, output and error of
.BR init .
.TP
.I /etc/rc
Boot and shutdown script runner.
.TP
.I /etc/rc.d/
Service scripts run by
.IR /etc/rc .
.TP
.I /etc/rc.subr
Shell helpers for the service scripts.
.TP
.I /sbin/getty
Program run on each terminal line.
.TP
.IR /dev/tty1 ", " /dev/tty2 ", " /dev/tty3
Terminal lines served.
.TP
.I /var/run/utmp
Current logins; reset at boot.
.TP
.I /var/log/wtmp
Login, logout, boot and shutdown history.
.SH EXAMPLES
Reboot the system cleanly:
.EX
# kill \-USR2 1
.EE
.PP
Restart one service by hand:
.EX
# /etc/rc.d/35-telnetd restart
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR last (1),
.BR getty (8),
.BR halt (8),
.BR reboot (8),
.BR poweroff (8),
.BR syslogd (8),
.BR dhclient (8),
.BR atd (8),
.BR telnetd (8),
.BR sdm (8),
.BR reboot (2),
.BR utmp (5)
.SH BUGS
There is no
.IR inittab :
the set of terminal lines is compiled in, so the getty on
.I /dev/tty1
cannot be turned off when
.BR sdm (8)
uses the same console, and there are no run levels.
.PP
.B SIGHUP
stays ignored in the processes
.B init
starts, since only
.BR SIGTERM ,
.BR SIGINT ,
.B SIGQUIT
and
.B SIGCHLD
are reset in the getty child and nothing is reset in the
.I /etc/rc
child.
